# ---------------------------------------------------------------- database
# Local development defaults to a SQLite file at ./data/store.db and needs
# nothing set here.
#
# Vercel's filesystem is ephemeral and read-only at runtime, so a deployed
# instance MUST point at a hosted database. Turso has a free tier and speaks
# the same protocol:
#
#   npx turso db create foundry
#   npx turso db show foundry --url
#   npx turso db tokens create foundry
#
# DATABASE_URL=libsql://foundry-you.turso.io
# DATABASE_AUTH_TOKEN=your-token

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- payments
# Leave these unset and checkout runs in simulation mode: orders complete
# instantly and are labelled "simulated" in the order history, so the whole
# flow works before you have a Stripe account.
#
# Set them and checkout redirects to Stripe. Fulfilment happens in the webhook
# (/api/webhook/stripe), not on the success redirect, so a buyer closing the
# tab still gets their download.
#
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- email
# Receipts and password-reset links. With no key set, messages are logged to
# the console and recorded in the `emails` table, so both flows work in
# development and are visible in the admin mail log.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_...
# EMAIL_FROM=Foundry <hello@yourdomain.com>

# -------------------------------------------------------------------- admin
# Comma-separated addresses allowed to open /admin. Access is an environment
# allowlist rather than a database flag, so a compromised account cannot
# promote itself. Unset means nobody is an admin.
#
# ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- firebase
# Firebase Analytics. A web config is public by design — it ships in the
# JavaScript bundle of every Firebase web app and identifies the project
# rather than authenticating it. What protects your data is your Firestore /
# Realtime Database security rules, so review those before the project holds
# anything real.
#
# Copy these from Firebase console → Project settings → Your apps.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_SENDER_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=
#
# Moving accounts and orders onto Firestore needs a SERVICE ACCOUNT key, which
# is a real secret — never commit it or paste it into chat. Generate it at
# Firebase console → Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new
# private key, then add the JSON as a single Vercel environment variable:
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account",...}

# ------------------------------------------------- firebase as the database
# Setting BOTH of these switches accounts, carts, orders, reviews and the mail
# log onto Firebase Realtime Database. Leave them unset and the app uses
# libSQL (DATABASE_URL above) instead. Nothing else changes.
#
# The service account is a REAL SECRET — never commit it. Generate it at
# Firebase console -> Project settings -> Service accounts -> Generate new
# private key, then paste the entire JSON file as one variable:
#
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT={"type":"service_account","project_id":"...",...}
# FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=https://your-project-default-rtdb.firebaseio.com
#
# Lock the database down first. Server access goes through the service
# account, which bypasses rules, so no client needs direct access:
#   { "rules": { ".read": false, ".write": false } }
